WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 85: The Great Dismal Swamp Maroons: The Secret City in the Morass

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.640
[Rebecca] Sprawled across the border of Southeast Virginia and Northeast North Carolina lies the Great Dismal Swamp.

4
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:18.880
[Rebecca] A million acres of impenetrable peat bogs, venomous snakes, black bears, and tangled cypress knees.

5
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.120
[Rebecca] To white planters and slave catchers, it was an unnavigable nightmare.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] But for generations, between the 17th century and the Civil War, it was something else entirely.

7
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.920
[Rebecca] A thriving, secret sanctuary of freedom.

8
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:49.520
[Rebecca] Thousands of self-liberated enslaved people, known as maroons, fled into the heart of the morass, building permanent settlements on elevated timber hammocks, cultivating gardens, raising families, and defying the chattel slavery of the American South for nearly two centuries.

9
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:56.080
[Rebecca] George brings us the forgotten story of the dismal swamp maroons.

10
00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:06.880
[George] In colonial Virginia, the Great Dismal Swamp was an intimidating expanse of primeval wilderness covering over 2,000 square miles between Norfolk and Albemarle Sound.

11
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:21.440
[George] The vast wetland was characterized by tea-colored acidic black water, dense cane breaks ten feet high, and ancient bald cypress trees draped in Spanish moss whose knobby wooden knees jutted from the peat like subterranean daggers.

12
00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:33.360
[George] Summer brought suffocating humidity, malaria-carrying mosquitoes and swarms of biting flies, while treacherous sinkholes and peat quagmires could swallow a horse to its shoulders in seconds.

13
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:46.800
[George] Early colonial surveyors, including William Biad II in 1728, viewed the swamp with unmitigated horror, describing it as a foul, dreadful morass where no civilized human being could survive.

14
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.360
[George] Yet beneath this forbidding exterior lay a hidden ecological sanctuary.

15
00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:09.400
[George] Scattered throughout the interior were elevated hammocks or mezzoic islands, sand ridges and ancient dunes rising several feet above the water level, forested with sweet gum, red maple, and oak trees that provided dry, fertile ground completely invisible from the swamp's outer perimeter.

16
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:21.640
[George] Indigenous peoples had utilized these secluded islands as seasonal hunting camps for thousands of years, creating overgrown paths and subtle waterways known only to those who lived in harmony with the wetlands.

17
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:35.560
[George] Surveyors in the 18th century warned that any traveler who strayed from marked blazes risked perishing in bottomless quagmires where peat fires burned underground for years, releasing sulfuric smoke that choked the forest air.

18
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:50.040
[George] Indigenous oral histories celebrated the swamp as a sacred boundary realm where spirits resided in the mist shrouded waters, teaching early European and African arrivals how to harvest edible cattail roots and avoid treacherous sinkholes.

19
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:59.080
[George] Dense canopies of Atlantic white cedar blocked the sun, creating an eerie perpetual twilight where navigating required reading moss patterns.

20
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:12.680
[George] Beginning in the late 17th century, as Virginia's plantation economy codified hereditary chattel slavery, enslaved Africans began looking toward the great dismal swamp as their ultimate fortress of liberation.

21
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:25.960
[George] Unlike fugitives who attempted the perilous, thousandmile journey north to Canada, runaways who reached the dismal swamp needed only to travel twenty or thirty miles from the tobacco and cotton plantations of the tidewater.

22
00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:40.360
[George] Slave catchers and bloodhound packs routinely pursued runaways to the swamp's muddy perimeter, but refused to venture into the tangled interior, terrified of quicksand, venomous cottonmouth water moccasins, and armed resistance.

23
00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:51.360
[George] Slaves who slipped into the blackwater used swamp water to mask their scent from hound dogs, wading waist deep through winding creeks and thick reeds until they vanished into the primeval gloom.

24
00:03:51.600 --> 00:04:01.760
[George] For an enslaved person facing the brutal lash, forced family separation, and lifelong servitude, the harsh wilderness of the swamp was infinitely preferable.

25
00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.360
[George] Within its trackless borders, they were no longer property.

26
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.720
[George] They were free human beings master of their own destiny.

27
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:22.960
[George] Plantation owners posted runaway slave notices in colonial gazettes, offering rewards of up to fifty pounds for runaways believed to be harboring in the dismal swamp, yet few professional slave hunters possessed the courage to pursue them.

28
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:35.760
[George] Runaway families traveled by night, wading through freezing streams to prevent bloodhounds from catching their trail, guided by the North Star shining brightly above the tangled canopy of the primeval cypress grove.

29
00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:44.960
[George] Bounty hunters who ventured near the swamp perimeter often returned empty-handed, reporting that unseen sentries watched from behind Spanish moss.

30
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:53.760
[George] For over two centuries, white society believed that runaways in the swamp were solitary, starving vagabonds living in miserable squalor.

31
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.880
[George] But groundbreaking 21st century archaeological excavations led by Dr.

32
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.240
[George] Daniel Sayers revealed an astonishing reality.

33
00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:09.040
[George] The maroons established complex, permanent, and highly organized communities that endured for generations.

34
00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:20.480
[George] On elevated sand islands covering several acres, maroons constructed sturdy log cabins with bark roofs and packed clay floors, organized around central communal courtyards.

35
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:31.480
[George] They cleared fields to cultivate corn, squash and beans, harvested wild cranberries and edible roots, and caught fish, turtles and wild game in the pristine waters.

36
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:41.800
[George] To avoid detection, they strictly avoided clearing the perimeter trees, maintaining a natural green camouflage that shielded their villages from aerial and surface view.

37
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:48.440
[George] Because iron tools and nails were scarce, maroons demonstrated extraordinary technical ingenuity.

38
00:05:48.440 --> 00:06:00.840
[George] Manufacturing their own stone tools, carved wooden bowls, and woven reed baskets, creating an entirely autonomous, self-sufficient material culture deep in the heart of the American South.

39
00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:14.920
[George] Archaeological excavations at interior swamp sites recovered quartz tools, hand-carved tobacco pipes, and stone fire hearths, providing physical proof of settled domestic family life that defied every colonial law.

40
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:28.040
[George] The communal cabins were constructed without iron nails, using interlocking notched logs and cedar bark ties that blended seamlessly into the surrounding maritime forest, completely invisible from fifty yards away.

41
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:37.320
[George] Archaeologists uncovered elevated clay mounds carefully engineered to stay dry during floods, proving sophisticated long-term settlement planning.

42
00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:47.400
[George] Perhaps the most extraordinary aspect of the dismal swamp maroons was the emergence of generational families who lived and died without ever setting foot in white society.

43
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:59.080
[George] By the early 19th century, hundreds of maroon children were born within the swamp, growing up entirely outside the reach of the slaveholder's whip, the courthouse register, or the auction block.

44
00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:06.280
[George] These swamp born generations possessed an intimate, instinctual mastery of the wetlands that surpassed any outsider.

45
00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:16.480
[George] They could navigate pitch black waterways by the stars, red subtle animal trails through the reeds, and move through waste-deep bogs without leaving a trace of disturbance.

46
00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:30.640
[George] Elders passed down oral histories, West African spiritual traditions and practical survival skills, creating a distinct, resilient African American culture rooted in communal solidarity and absolute independence.

47
00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:41.200
[George] For these children of the swamp, slavery was not a personal memory, but a distant, terrifying empire that lurked beyond the perimeter of their forested island paradise.

48
00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:55.520
[George] Generations of children raised on the interior islands learned to harvest wild honey, identify medicinal herbs from marsh plants, and craft canoes from hollowed cypress logs, building a rich folklore of resilience and survival.

49
00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:08.880
[George] Elders instructed young hunters in the silent use of hand-carved wooden bows and bone-tipped fish spears, ensuring that meat was obtained for the village without the concussive gunshot report that might alert perimeter slave patrols.

50
00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:17.360
[George] Mothers passed down ancestral herbal remedies, teaching children which wild roots provided nourishment and how to treat swamp fevers.

51
00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:30.080
[George] While interior maroon settlements remained completely isolated, a secondary group of swamp dwellers, known as outliers, established a thriving, clandestine economic network with the outside world.

52
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:45.920
[George] In the late 18th century, speculative enterprises, including the dismal Swamp Land Company founded by George Washington, began logging the swamp's valuable Atlantic white cedar and bald cypress trees to manufacture rotresistant roofing shingles.

53
00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:57.040
[George] Planters hired out enslaved lumberjacks, who were left unsupervised in remote logging camps deep in the swamp for months at a time, tasked with meeting strict shingle quotas.

54
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:02.760
[George] Maroon communities formed mutually beneficial economic alliances with these enslaved woodsmen.

55
00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:11.080
[George] Maroons secretly cut thousands of high-quality cedar shingles and delivered them to the loggers, who turned them over to white overseers.

56
00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:22.840
[George] In exchange, the lumberjacks provided the Maroons with vital manufactured goods iron axes, cooking pots, gun flints, powder, lead shot, salt, and cloth.

57
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:35.560
[George] This shadow economy generated thousands of dollars in illicit commerce, allowing Maroons to acquire essential tools while enabling enslaved lumberjacks to earn extra cash to purchase freedom for their loved ones.

58
00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:49.000
[George] Enslaved shingle cutters who operated on the swamp outer canals developed secret whistle codes and drum beats to communicate with interior marons, warning of approaching slave patrols and arranging nocturnal trade meetings.

59
00:09:49.000 --> 00:10:01.720
[George] The shadow trade along the timber canals provided the Maroons with iron cooking pots, salt and sewing needles, while enabling enslaved lumberjacks to buy freedom for their children through clandestine earnings.

60
00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:11.000
[George] The secret trading network between maroons and canal workers circulated iron goods, grain, salt, and news of abolitionist movements.

61
00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:20.440
[George] The existence of the great dismal swamp maroons cast a terrifying psychological shadow over white plantation owners across Virginia and North Carolina.

62
00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:32.760
[George] Whenever slave rebellions were rumored or planned, most notably Nat Turner's bloody Southampton insurrection in 1831, which occurred barely 40 miles west, white authorities panicked.

63
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:41.480
[George] Convinced that Turner was marching toward the Dismal Swamp to join forces with thousands of armed Maroons and establish a sovereign black republic.

64
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:53.200
[George] Armed militias raided the swamp's fringes, burning isolated huts and murdering suspected runaways, but the fortified interior settlements remained entirely undiscovered and unbreached.

65
00:10:53.200 --> 00:11:00.960
[George] When the Civil War erupted in 1861, the Dismal Swamp became a critical operational base for the Union war effort.

66
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:08.320
[George] Maroons served as expert scouts, guides and intelligence couriers for federal forces operating in coastal Virginia.

67
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:20.800
[George] Hundreds of Maroon men emerged from the Cypress groves to enlist in the United States colored troops, taking up rifles to fight for the total eradication of the chattel system that had driven their ancestors into the morass.

68
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:34.400
[George] During the War of 1812 and the Civil War, Federal commanders recognized that the Maroons possessed incomparable tactical knowledge of coastal waterways, employing them as intelligence couriers and scouts behind Confederate lines.

69
00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:47.280
[George] The myth of an armed Maroon kingdom deep in the morass terrified tidewater planters for generations, prompting Virginia lawmakers to pass draconian patrol laws that failed completely to penetrate the swamp interior.

70
00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:56.320
[George] During the Civil War, escaped slaves hidden in the Dismal Swamp provided invaluable topographical intelligence to Union coastal gunboats.

71
00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:07.440
[George] With the ratification of the Thirteenth Amendment in 1865, the institution of slavery was abolished, and the historic necessity of the Dismal Swamp Sanctuary came to an end.

72
00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:22.480
[George] Many Marons gradually left the interior islands to reunite with freed family members in Norfolk, Portsmouth, and Elizabeth City, integrating into postwar African American communities as skilled carpenters, farmers, and community leaders.

73
00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:35.000
[George] Today, over 112,000 acres are preserved as the Great Dismal Swamp National Wildlife Refuge, a recognized site on the National Park Service's underground railroad network to freedom.

74
00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:56.760
[George] As sunlight filters through the moss draped branches and water lilies bloom upon the mirror like surface of Lake Drummond, the wetlands preserve the sacred memory of thousands of courageous men and women who preferred the perils of the wild over the chains of bondage, proving that the human spirit will build a citadel of liberty in the darkest morass rather than submit to tyranny.

75
00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:10.440
[George] Today, the preserved wilderness of the great dismal swamp stands as hallowed ground in African American history, commemorating thousands of brave souls who chose dangerous freedom in the wilderness over a lifetime of slavery.

76
00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:24.600
[George] The story of the Great Dismal Swamp Maroons reveals that the history of American slavery was also a continuous history of courageous, organized resistance, where freedom was seized through collective ingenuity and unshakable resolve.

77
00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:34.520
[George] Today, the historical legacy of the Great Dismal Swamp Maroons serves as an enduring testament to the human drive for freedom against impossible odds.

78
00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:45.400
[Rebecca] The Great Dismal Swamp Maroons built an invisible kingdom of freedom in the heart of the slave South, an enduring testament to the unconquerable human desire for liberty.

79
00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:46.760
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

80
00:13:46.760 --> 00:14:01.320
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we stand beneath the bombardment of Fort McHenry for the true story of the Star Spangled Fair.

